WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Configuration bugs are often boring and dangerous at the same time.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.520
A missing session secret may trigger fallback behavior.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:15.100
A value that should be false may be interpreted as true because any non-empty string was treated as enabled.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.280
A rate limit may be disabled because a number failed to parse.

5
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.180
A URL may point at the wrong environment.

6
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:30.040
These failures are preventable when configuration is validated before the application handles traffic or before a sensitive feature is used.

7
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:43.440
Validate required values, types, formats, allowed values, ranges, URLs, ports, booleans, durations, file paths, environment names, and security modes.

8
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:48.900
Centralized schemas or validation logic can make expected configuration visible and testable.

9
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.560
Avoid stringly typed assumptions.

10
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.180
For booleans, parse accepted values explicitly.

11
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.400
For durations and sizes, define units.

12
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.660
For origins, endpoints, and modes, use allowlists that match the business and deployment model.

13
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.620
Fail closed when security-critical configuration is missing, malformed, or unsafe.

14
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.160
A startup failure should be clear enough for operators to fix quickly, but it should not reveal secrets.

15
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:22.740
Validation reduces drift between environments because it catches mistakes before runtime traffic depends on them.

16
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:31.480
It also creates evidence: the team can see which configuration was expected, whether it was present, and whether it passed the checks required for that environment.
